133: {date:'GOTHAM CITY · THAT NIGHT',title:'The promise',text:'<p>The gunshot cracks across the street. Thomas and Martha fall beneath the streetlamp. Bruce is left kneeling beside them, surrounded by a city that keeps moving but will never look the same to him again.</p><p>In the weeks that follow, adults speak softly around him. They tell him he is safe now, that the police will find the killer, that time will make the pain smaller. None of those words can bring his parents back. Bruce learns that helplessness can feel louder than any siren.</p><p>Grief hardens into a promise—not a plan yet, only a vow he will carry into every room: <strong>no one else should have to feel this alone.</strong></p>'},
134: {date:'WAYNE MANOR · THE YEARS AFTER',title:'A house full of echoes',text:'<p>The manor is enormous, but after the funeral it feels too quiet. Bruce grows up among portraits, locked rooms, and the careful kindness of the family’s longtime butler, Alfred Pennyworth. Alfred makes breakfast, remembers birthdays, and never asks Bruce to pretend he is all right.</p><p>Bruce studies, travels, and searches for a way to understand the violence that took his parents. He learns how fear works, how people survive, and how easily a powerful family can mistake its own comfort for the health of its city.</p><p>The promise stays with him. It changes shape as he does: first a child’s wish, then a question, and finally a responsibility he cannot hand to anyone else.</p>'},
