  Line 138: {date:'GOTHAM CITY · THAT NIGHT',title:'The promise',text:'<p>The gunshot cracks across the street. Thomas and Martha fall beneath the streetlamp. Bruce is left kneeling beside them, surrounded by a city that keeps moving but will never look the same to him again.</p><p>In the weeks that follow, adults speak softly around him. They tell him he is safe now, that the police will find the killer, that time will make the pain smaller. None of those words can bring his parents back. Bruce learns that helplessness can feel louder than any siren.</p><p>Grief hardens into a promise—not a plan yet, only a vow he will carry into every room: <strong>no one else should have to feel this alone.</strong></p>'},

  Line 139: {date:'WAYNE MANOR · THE YEARS AFTER',title:'A house full of echoes',text:'<p>The manor is enormous, but after the funeral it feels too quiet. Bruce grows up among portraits, locked rooms, and the careful kindness of the family’s longtime butler, Alfred Pennyworth. Alfred makes breakfast, remembers birthdays, and never asks Bruce to pretend he is all right.</p><p>Bruce studies, travels, and searches for a way to understand the violence that took his parents. He learns how fear works, how people survive, and how easily a powerful family can mistake its own comfort for the health of its city.</p><p>The promise stays with him. It changes shape as he does: first a child’s wish, then a question, and finally a responsibility he cannot hand to anyone else.</p>'},

  Line 141: {date:'GOTHAM CITY · 2009',title:'The cost of hope',text:'<p>A year later, a terrible incident leaves Harvey Dent’s face scarred and his life changed. The city’s bright public hope is suddenly vulnerable to pain, anger, and the cruel appetite of headlines. Bruce watches the damage from a distance and recognizes the same old feeling: the moment when safety proves it was never guaranteed.</p><p>He retreats from the parties and the easy explanations. With a bag in hand, he descends beneath Wayne Manor into a forgotten cave. The stone is cold, the passages are rough, and the darkness seems almost honest. Here, at least, nobody asks him to smile for a photograph.</p><p>For a while, Bruce tells himself solitude is the same thing as purpose.</p>'},

  Line 143: {date:'WAYNE CAVE · THREE MONTHS LATER',title:'The long quiet',text:'<p>Days pass. Bruce returns to the cave with tools, then with plans, then with nothing but questions. He measures the cavern and marks out possible work areas. He tries to turn the rock into a headquarters by force of will, but every task exposes another one he does not know how to do.</p><p>Above him, the manor continues its routines. Below, the cave answers with drips of water and the occasional flutter of wings. Bruce begins to understand that a mission built alone may only become another way to hide.</p><p>One evening he hears footsteps at the entrance. The same old man has come back. This time, he is carrying a thermos and a small stack of notes.</p>'},

